WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:04.680
It is empowering, or at least therapeutic, in these dark times to imagine what a

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:09.240
time literate society might look like. In
his last public interview Kurt Vonnegut

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:14.040
said, "I'll tell you one thing no cabinet
has ever had is a secretary of the

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.830
future. And there are no plans at all for
my grandchildren and my

00:00:16.830 --> 00:00:20.699
great-grandchildren." Let us adopt
Vonnegut's suggestion as our first

00:00:20.699 --> 00:00:24.600
proposal: a representative for the yet to
be born to serve among the top advisors

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:29.070
to the president. The Department of the
Future would set in motion a realignment

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:33.420
of priorities in all aspects of society.
And now we should cue some triumphant

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:39.239
music, I think. The secretary will declare
some new national holidays: Aldo Leopold,

00:00:39.239 --> 00:00:44.420
Rachel Carson, or Chief Seattle
Days to honor great long-term thinkers.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:48.930
Photosynthesis and Carbonate Rock Week—a time to celebrate the mighty legions of

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:52.469
coccolithophores and others who have kept the planet from becoming a runaway

00:00:52.469 --> 00:00:57.480
greenhouse. Resource conservation would again become a core value and patriotic

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:02.160
virtue. Tax incentives and subsidies
would be rebalanced toward rewarding

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.479
long-term stewardship over short-term
exploitation. Putting a price on carbon

00:01:06.479 --> 00:01:10.560
will help us get a grip on our fossil
fuel addictions, sober up, and let us

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:14.340
prepare for the natural disasters that
will happen without our assistance, like

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.400
the hundreds of large earthquakes that
will happen around the globe in the next

00:01:17.400 --> 00:01:22.009
century, rather than expending resources
on self-created climate catastrophes.

00:01:22.009 --> 00:01:25.979
Poverty and class-based disparity of
opportunity would be recognized as

00:01:25.979 --> 00:01:29.759
problems with deep historical roots that
cannot be solved without sustained

00:01:29.759 --> 00:01:34.020
commitment over commensurate timescales into the future. Public school teachers

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:37.890
and others whose work represents an
investment in the future would be paid

00:01:37.890 --> 00:01:41.329
well and held in high esteem.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:50.320
Geology would be fully integrated into
science curricula, perhaps serving as a

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:54.399
capstone course in which students would apply physics, chemistry and biology to

00:01:54.399 --> 00:01:58.869
the immensely complex earth system. With a solid understanding of how the planet

00:01:58.869 --> 00:02:02.979
works, students would go on to become
educated voters who would hold public

00:02:02.979 --> 00:02:07.110
officials accountable for wise governance of water, land and air.

00:02:07.110 --> 00:02:11.200
Legislators, governors, and mayors who
embrace the Seven Generation Principle

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.550
would point proudly to what they are
doing and be reelected by grateful

00:02:15.550 --> 00:02:19.750
constituents. More generally, schools
would help develop children's knowledge

00:02:19.750 --> 00:02:24.250
of and appetite for History and Natural
History, instilling in them a deep

00:02:24.250 --> 00:02:30.420
instinct for their place in time and a
keen curiosity to understand more.

